#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192331 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3fe3596-340e-5155-bed3-cf25f5843d70" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Two-Dimensional Space"}] :question/id #uuid "32f7b0e6-5a68-52ab-9d86-ea71387cfb77" :question/math-academy-id "e-10975" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045249/assets/72329ee334988361174013e7df57452851cd826acf2ffc8b4cead6d750683e73.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of $\\mathbb{R}^2?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents the whole plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ without the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin. Therefore, it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n- Diagram II is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n- Diagram III is a parabola that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin. Therefore, it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192332 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces"}] :question/id #uuid "554fc7b1-62ca-5189-bda1-712f518ad617" :question/math-academy-id "e-10976" :question/problem "Consider the subsets $\\; A=\\{ \\langle x, y, z \\rangle \\in \\mathbb{R}^3 : x^2=y \\}\\;$ and $\\; B= \\{ \\langle 3-t, -3+t, -6+2t \\rangle : t \\in \\mathbb{R} \\}.\\;$ Which of them is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it is not closed under scalar multiplication. For example, the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045249/assets/b0bc18bcfd9d61510bed4ec4126688b599f5d3e6dbc883394ef7d5acbcf935f6.png) belongs to $A,$ but the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045249/assets/3f87fe8f3e9e256453a5d60981170b1655286081f9987bbeb7efc7624f6c6749.png) does not lie in $A$ (because it does not satisfy $x^2=y$).\n\n- The set $B$ represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. If the origin belongs to this subset, we would have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045249/assets/75f1370c0eaafdcab1aa8e4cc673dbe6f71c61a0ad9f69a437ff9d8f4b115c42.png)\nwhich gives us\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3-t=0 \\\\ -3+t=0 \\\\ -6+2t=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $t=3$ is the solution to the three equations. So, if we choose $t=3,$ then we find that $\\mathbf{0} \\in B.$ Therefore, $B$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192333 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a8dbbab-2dea-5fff-86f0-3411f5405ee9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/id #uuid "6e80df98-c67c-5f27-a44b-d0bc8f681ac6" :question/math-academy-id "e-10979" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045249/assets/c66cdc8bbddfb00ce69f843b1ce1d12116b2dd82312a34abafa0fb13907110cf.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I  represents a plane that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of  $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n- Diagram II  represents a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is also a subspace of  $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045249/assets/a80f685053d7ac3f868728be13b5651c15bfb6c8f8f4394df75256b750a67485.png)  clearly lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347453 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a8dbbab-2dea-5fff-86f0-3411f5405ee9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c719ef3-3d87-51c3-94ce-1bf565d416a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347455} {:answer/id #uuid "4f874e82-b5c5-5eaf-8c01-7793d41da213" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347456} {:answer/id #uuid "9ec218a5-4eeb-511f-ba57-82240e8cebcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347457} {:answer/id #uuid "97ee61bd-4886-5a11-81e9-a51d6ea76e28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347458} {:answer/id #uuid "a4ce0839-27f9-5f7a-b696-7ada00f85fd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ec218a5-4eeb-511f-ba57-82240e8cebcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347457} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad9fbaac-d908-5772-b38d-b6c7cc3695fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "def845b9-0c5b-5d87-865d-4200537b5ccb" :question/math-academy-id "q-110449" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045249/assets/8c695ea0cb09807c22fa655182945e838a7c5e454680176add27748945d143e5.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$  clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$  clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a line passing through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of  ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347460 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a8dbbab-2dea-5fff-86f0-3411f5405ee9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca8a55b1-2213-5139-a8ec-c84996f2d599" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347462} {:answer/id #uuid "fd1cd958-bbfe-545b-83be-728ba3b3b098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347463} {:answer/id #uuid "1248c104-5de3-5388-a906-bf5403c89290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347464} {:answer/id #uuid "7e9a8774-4a22-5032-9c67-d7c9f5b2320a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347465} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d4814e-b0d3-579b-ac98-3162d4ed5a85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1248c104-5de3-5388-a906-bf5403c89290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347464} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bbb645b-9593-51a8-b46e-239ca2c044d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c26b72d3-fad6-5aa8-8bb7-425ec646f0f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-110597" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045249/assets/f2ed737550ddf5502d2a066cdc279285b624000a6c2ad8464496c122ad31a3ab.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ lies inside the given subset. But, \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$  clearly lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347467 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3fe3596-340e-5155-bed3-cf25f5843d70" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Two-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84151b21-6f86-5a8f-aa97-e624b37808e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347469} {:answer/id #uuid "fbda7dc0-e7f5-5bf8-a648-bceda7dad6b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347470} {:answer/id #uuid "6a914d9c-5401-5fa1-b1c5-9f0e1a1053c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347471} {:answer/id #uuid "6052102f-7783-591d-9aeb-d17a0642bc80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347472} {:answer/id #uuid "05413364-8482-521e-a9a6-20b4e06c8cd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6052102f-7783-591d-9aeb-d17a0642bc80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347472} :answer-field/id #uuid "c11c39a3-1a92-52c7-a05a-0f6c1920d5f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8507283e-b6bb-5932-a798-405f13041eec" :question/math-academy-id "q-110600" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045249/assets/815f5ce1dd5bf3bc30b7853b53cd97736ffeb8224f30bc864cc7e43e07bedb5a.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II represents a set that does not contain the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents the whole plane ${R}^{2}$ without the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram III is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347474 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "672bfc64-fc1b-5c48-97ab-eda6ca11974a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347476} {:answer/id #uuid "bf59cbe1-fcfa-5bbb-a07e-2e57ffc9ecc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347477} {:answer/id #uuid "eb99172f-5a1e-5b34-a0b5-8a275f34e668" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347478} {:answer/id #uuid "dc8e330c-accc-5a71-a770-4ad6fc6e6dc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347479} {:answer/id #uuid "542ec833-404f-5ad9-8e01-09c8df03924e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "542ec833-404f-5ad9-8e01-09c8df03924e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347480} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8879922-bb8a-5f15-ad55-70d6d2a97517" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2f7eefdd-4562-5d0a-9cb1-c1a07721f95b" :question/math-academy-id "q-110611" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $A={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:xyz=1}$\n- $B={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:x+y-z=0}$\n- $C={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:y=-1}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition $xyz=1$ since $0\\cdot 0\\cdot 0\\ne 1.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x+y-z=0$ since $0+0-0=0.$ So $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition $y=-1$ since $0\\ne -1.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347481 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18cc4f6d-6456-5a28-ac40-984297c1a92b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347483} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ecbd8d-4951-5118-b33f-625f599c022d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347484} {:answer/id #uuid "5ed40ab3-58a6-5278-9d32-2d5e7d05af6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347485} {:answer/id #uuid "8a7c89d0-eebd-53bf-a042-8105694164ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347486} {:answer/id #uuid "c8246e47-ca7b-59dc-ad57-d15f9cdaedb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8246e47-ca7b-59dc-ad57-d15f9cdaedb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347487} :answer-field/id #uuid "f28a8b77-8231-5720-9ee8-cabc25da699d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d2f09bfc-d5e1-5138-8cc9-33a8e30bda91" :question/math-academy-id "q-110760" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $A={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:x-4y=1}$\n- $B={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:x=-2y}$\n- $C={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}<0}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each subset.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. For instance, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $x-4y=1$ since $0-4\\cdot 0=0\\ne 1.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x=-2y$ since $0=-2\\cdot 0.$ So $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace. No point in ${R}^{3}$ satisfies the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}<0.$ Hence, $C=∅.$ The empty set is not a subspace since it does not contain the zero vector.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347488 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3fe3596-340e-5155-bed3-cf25f5843d70" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Two-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc785276-321d-51d0-92c7-e6d41fa4e652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347490} {:answer/id #uuid "dd5d7a67-ce02-5823-a0c2-95187e6f5c79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347491} {:answer/id #uuid "8a6714e6-5663-5d78-a769-df05ce5e33c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347492} {:answer/id #uuid "9283837b-242a-504a-a058-3bbdecfdaf06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347493} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a8f729-b2ec-590c-8169-e7cfa9f91e26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9283837b-242a-504a-a058-3bbdecfdaf06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347493} :answer-field/id #uuid "800e3389-116b-5358-908e-0ded7b08f434" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347489}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5dac79b-5bd7-5f29-894c-68471abee19e" :question/math-academy-id "q-158029" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045249/assets/7359571270dd1ce7df2b68d396e2c87cbf44bd7b18adf94bdb7ade4c02a8bc12.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a set that does not contain the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram I is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a set that does not contain the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace.  In this case, we have that the vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a8dbbab-2dea-5fff-86f0-3411f5405ee9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51a07276-6649-556e-9776-1ed251989a4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347497} {:answer/id #uuid "7733bf89-0d66-5aa2-bd08-d0af51fe077c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347498} {:answer/i