Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc2b0312-e1a3-58c2-b94e-0c67abde35c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347525} {:answer/id #uuid "aae593b1-e75d-5fec-9fd9-bca17dd77d2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347526} {:answer/id #uuid "1ff94b71-9f03-54aa-a3b3-54325a27df0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347527} {:answer/id #uuid "91fb9ec6-6879-50c1-a7aa-7d354a15aad9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347528} {:answer/id #uuid "d5ac0df7-7dce-58f4-843b-7cc6c9921a86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aae593b1-e75d-5fec-9fd9-bca17dd77d2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347526} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec52a9b5-d9f9-589a-b1ea-9aed3cc42a0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa9c6a5a-75da-5524-b31b-a7d0f8bee20d" :question/math-academy-id "q-35599" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $A={⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 0}$\n- $B={⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x=\\frac{1}{2}y+2}$\n- $C={0}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is a subspace. There is only one vector in ${R}^{2}$ that satisfies the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 0,$ namely, the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩.$ So, $A={0}$, which is a special subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $x=\\frac{1}{2}y+2$ since $0\\ne \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0+2.$\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. This subset has only one element, the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩,$ which is a special subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347530 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3fe3596-340e-5155-bed3-cf25f5843d70" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Two-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd277386-d90f-5ff0-b0eb-4ea1b93b1766" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347532} {:answer/id #uuid "2da251fc-63ec-55b2-80df-2661ea4c6ede" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347533} {:answer/id #uuid "d5368ab0-8ae9-589b-863f-36f997f2f7d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347534} {:answer/id #uuid "90aaf807-947a-52ea-bb57-030154d8bf8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347535} {:answer/id #uuid "8fe93e33-098b-513b-ae76-0596a5a0c13a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fe93e33-098b-513b-ae76-0596a5a0c13a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347536} :answer-field/id #uuid "47692804-81b4-54ad-b1d6-9b943810f370" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347531}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "985b4894-28d8-5dc3-a41a-ba282ffe63f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-35600" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045249/assets/ec79ffa15b3ed25d8f439f575e1cdffc3122ae1d82e1b55500a6e5e2e17cf4fc.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace.  Every subspace must contain the origin,  but from the diagram, we can easily see that the origin is not in the subset.\n\n- Diagram II represents a subspace. It represents the whole plane, which is considered a special subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents the whole plane ${R}^{2}$  without the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram III is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "283d9b5b-fa77-5dd1-917e-aeba63cc6db8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347539} {:answer/id #uuid "2da680ee-7912-5815-ab67-dc610d8c26f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347540} {:answer/id #uuid "19bbfa69-c2f1-574e-8f8b-32fb296a7af1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347541} {:answer/id #uuid "8473034b-5111-50da-a03f-842d6e9efe9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347542} {:answer/id #uuid "c166fdb4-a774-59a2-a9dc-2b1bd5315fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19bbfa69-c2f1-574e-8f8b-32fb296a7af1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347541} :answer-field/id #uuid "db84572d-151c-52ee-8d3d-8a3d74303cfa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "65e032cf-8dda-5eb9-b8c9-342357ab6106" :question/math-academy-id "q-74217" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $A={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:1-x-y=0}$\n- $B={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1}$\n- $C={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:2x+3y-z=0}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $1-x-y=0$ since $1-0-0\\ne 0.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace. Indeed, the vector $u=⟨1,0,0⟩$ is in $B$, but $2u=⟨2,0,0⟩$ is not in $B$, since ${2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}≰1.$ So, $B$ is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $2x+3y-z=0$ since $2\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 0-0=0.$ So $0$ lies in $C.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347544 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a21df31e-2fcb-580b-9c42-0723d6014f5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347546} {:answer/id #uuid "2324473b-3b0a-5672-b74a-70e479023dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347547} {:answer/id #uuid "4dc65f3f-f060-5c8f-8456-4043fa543989" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347548} {:answer/id #uuid "f2897c60-481b-52e0-b8b3-905e3398f8cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347549} {:answer/id #uuid "61307106-65e0-5ca5-8c49-99b4fe624265" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dc65f3f-f060-5c8f-8456-4043fa543989" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347548} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fda5663-6ca0-57e8-ba77-a2145fb06cb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cb5240e2-b91f-57ca-8218-7498b10038ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-74218" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $A={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1}$\n- $B={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:xyz=0}$\n- $C={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:y=-1}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ since ${0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}\\ne 1.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace because it is not closed under vector addition. Indeed, both $a=⟨1,0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨0,1,1⟩$ belong to $B$ but \n\n$$\na+b=⟨1,0,1⟩+⟨0,1,1⟩=⟨1,1,2⟩\n$$\n\n \ndoes not satisfy the condition $xyz=0$ since $1\\cdot 1\\cdot 2\\ne 0.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition $y=-1$ since $0\\ne -1.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None.\""}]}
